## 0. Structural Overview — Statistics and Analysis

The encoding module (`src/encoding.rs`, 101 lines) provides:

- An error type `EncodingError` wrapping `polynomial::PolynomialError`
  plus two decoding-specific variants (`ChunkIndexDecodingError`,
  `ChunkDataDecodingError`).
- A plain data struct `Chunk { index: u16, data: [u8; 32] }`.
- Two traits — `Encoder` and `Decoder` — defining the encoding/decoding
  API surface.
- Blanket `Option<T>` implementations of both traits, which lift the
  inner `T`-level methods through the `Some` constructor using a
  take/unwrap/delegate/re-wrap idiom necessitated by hax's inability
  to handle mutable references returned by `Option::take`.
- A `From<PolynomialError> for EncodingError` conversion enabling the
  `?` operator to automatically lift polynomial-layer errors.

### 0a. Core Definitions

| Definition | Source | Description |
|------------|--------|-------------|
| `EncodingError` | `src/encoding.rs:8–16` | Error enum: `PolynomialError(PolynomialError)`, `ChunkIndexDecodingError`, `ChunkDataDecodingError`. |
| `Chunk` | `src/encoding.rs:24–28` | Plain struct: `{ index: u16, data: [u8; 32] }` — a single encoding chunk. |
| `Encoder` | `src/encoding.rs:31–37` | Trait with two methods: `encode_bytes(msg) → Result<Self, EncodingError>`, `next_chunk(&mut self) → Chunk`. |
| `Decoder` | `src/encoding.rs:40–47` | Trait with three methods: `new(len_bytes) → Result<Self, EncodingError>`, `add_chunk(&mut self, chunk)`, `decoded_message(&self) → Option<Vec<u8>>`. |

### 0b. The `Option<T>` Blanket Lifting Pattern

All `Option<T>` blanket implementations share the same structural
idiom:

1. `core.option.Option.take self` — pure decomposition of the option.
2. `core.option.Option.unwrap` — partial projection (requires
   `isSome`).
3. Delegation to the inner `T`-level trait method.
4. Re-injection into `Some` (or wrapping the constructor result in
   `Some`).

This pattern introduces no new mathematical content. The `Option`
layer merely repackages inputs and outputs, and the spec of each
outer method carries no extra content beyond that of the inner
`T`-level method.

The Rust source includes `hax_lib::fstar!` calls in the `next_chunk`,
`add_chunk`, and `decoded_message` blanket implementations that assume
the inner trait preconditions hold on the unwrapped value. These are
opaque hax assumptions and correspond to the `#[hax_lib::requires]`
annotations on the trait methods.

