## 3. Function Descriptions

### Error Conversion

- **`{impl From<PolynomialError> for EncodingError}::from`** [F1] —
  Wraps a `polynomial::PolynomialError` in the
  `EncodingError::PolynomialError` constructor, enabling the `?`
  operator to automatically lift polynomial-layer errors into
  encoding-layer errors. Always succeeds (no panic).

### Encoder Blanket Implementations (`Option<T>` where `T: Encoder`)

All `Option<T>` encoder methods share the take/unwrap/delegate/re-wrap
idiom:

1. `core.option.Option.take self` — pure decomposition.
2. `core.option.Option.unwrap` — partial projection (requires
   `isSome`).
3. Delegation to the inner `T`-level trait method.
4. Re-injection into `Some`.

- **`{impl Encoder for Option<T>}::encode_bytes(msg)`** [F2] —
  Delegates to `T::encode_bytes(msg)` and wraps the successful result
  in `Some`. If the inner call returns an error, the error is
  propagated unchanged. Simpler than the mutable-reference methods
  because `encode_bytes` is a static constructor.

- **`{impl Encoder for Option<T>}::next_chunk(&mut self)`** [F3] —
  Requires `self.is_some()`. Extracts the inner encoder via
  `take`/`unwrap`, delegates to `T::next_chunk`, re-wraps the updated
  encoder in `Some`, and returns the chunk. The Rust source includes a
  `hax_lib::fstar!` call that assumes the inner `next_chunk`
  precondition holds on the unwrapped value.

### Decoder Blanket Implementations (`Option<T>` where `T: Decoder`)

- **`{impl Decoder for Option<T>}::new(len_bytes)`** [F4] —
  Delegates to `T::new(len_bytes)` and wraps the successful result in
  `Some`. Static constructor — no take/unwrap needed.

- **`{impl Decoder for Option<T>}::add_chunk(&mut self, chunk)`** [F5] —
  Requires `self.is_some()`. Uses take/unwrap/delegate/re-wrap on
  `T::add_chunk`. The Rust source includes a `hax_lib::fstar!` call
  that assumes the inner `add_chunk` precondition holds.

- **`{impl Decoder for Option<T>}::decoded_message(&self)`** [F6] —
  Requires `self.is_some()`. Delegates to `T::decoded_message` on the
  unwrapped inner value via `as_ref().unwrap()`. Unlike the mutable
  methods, this is a shared-reference call, so no take/re-wrap is
  needed — only unwrap/delegate. The Rust source includes a
  `hax_lib::fstar!` call that assumes the inner `decoded_message`
  precondition holds.

